    summary: "The forces on a train (tractive effort within the adhesion, running resistance, gradients and curves, brakes), its run along a line as fast as its traction and the limits allow, braking for lower limits and the stop, its running time, the energy it takes, coasting before the stop, the forces in its couplings and its load limit; slippery rails as a disruption.",
    description: "trainModel makes the figures of a consist the running dynamics need: the mass and the mass factor of the rotating masses, the tractive effort and power of the traction vehicles that can draw the line's power, the mass on their driven axles, the regenerative brake force, the auxiliary power, the brakes, and every vehicle's mass, mass factor and Strahl coefficient. runProfile integrates a run along a line (sections with their speed limit, gradient, curve and rails) in steps of distance: forward as fast as the tractive effort allows, backward braking with the service brake from the stop and from each lower limit, the lower of both; a limit holds until the end of the train has passed it. The times, phases and energy follow from the speeds; a train without vehicles, or one whose traction cannot move it, does not run (its time is infinite). coastingRun finds the earliest point to coast from that keeps the running time within the supplement, by halving with runs in coarser steps, then checks it at full resolution and coasts later where the run would take longer; drivingStyles shares the run as fast as possible among the supplements. The Minden formula gives full braking distances, the speed a braking table allows and the brake percentage needed. Slippery rails (low-adhesion) on one track of a platform make its trains come in and leave slower by approachFactor of the train at it (the effect slow of the services; worked out once per consist and condition) and set the rails' condition of its track for the cards.",
